eft/right bias\n        v) Gaussian profile centered at the middle\n\n   b) Use the following structure for optimization:\n        - At each iteration, calculate the convolution and identify peaks\n        - For each peak, temporarily reduce coefficients in its vicinity while maintaining sum\n        - The new sequence must be compared to the old for improved parallel convolution\n        - Use fast Fourier transforms to handle convolution for n=384\n\n   c) Pseudo-code:\n        import numpy as np\n        from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n        n = 384\n        S_val = np.sqrt(2 * n)   # normalization constant\n\n        def generate_initial_profiles():\n            # Generate various initial candidate sequences\n            base_right_edge = ...   # left edge heavy\n            base_left_edge = ...     # right edge heavy\n            gaussian = ...           # two-sided Gaussian\n            uniform = ...              # uniform decay\n\n            return [base_right_edge, base_left_edge, gaussian, uniform]\n\n        def peak_location(conv_sequence):\n            # Find the position(s) of the maximum convolution element\n            # These are initial points for our descent\n            n_peak = ...\n            for idx in range(n_peak):\n                ...\n\n        def optimize_sequence(initial_seq):\n            # Conduct a local optimization with projected gradient\n            sequence_normalized = initial_seq / sum(initial_seq) * S_val\n            step_sizes = [0.1, 0.5, 0.8]   # gradually reduce optimizations\n            ...\n\n        def evolve_sequence(candidate):\n            # Apply frequency-domain shifts to the step function\n            # Make sure the convolution becomes flatter\n            ...\n\n        best_sequence = generate_initial_profiles()[0]\n        best_objective = float('inf')\n\n        for initial in generate_initial_profiles():\n            initial_seq = normalize_sequence(initial, S_val)\n            obj_current = evaluate_sequence(initial_seq)\n            if obj_current < best_objective:\n                best_objective = obj_current\n                best_sequence = initial_seq\n\n        while ...:\n            # Update the sequence based on current peaks\n            # Penalize high-energy peaks and reward smooth areas\n\n            # Apply gradient descent with momentum\n            # The norm sum should remain >=0.01\n\n            ... # Update until gradient override stabilizes or time ends\n\n        # Return best_sequence found\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   We'll explore:\n   - Several base profiles (right, left, uniform, Gaussian, asymmetric)\n   - Adaptive step sizes for gradient descent: decreasing over iterations\n   - Different relaxation factors for momentum optimization\n   - Varying degree of asymmetry in the edge profiles\n\n   The code will automatically select the best profile by comparing their objectives.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - With n=384 fixed, the convolution time is manageable with FFT\n   - A budget of 1.5027 must be tracked: the evaluation function returns the ratio value\n   - Avoid anomalies: maintain non-negative elements in the returned sequence\n\n   We assume:\n     - That the local optimizer (via gradient descent) can make 100 iterations\n     - That FFT convolution is limited to 384-length sequences only\n\n   During optimization:\n     - Use adaptive trial lengths to keep within time bounds\n     - Ensure the evaluated sequence has positive-sum and non-negative elements\n\n   Fallback: if no valid sequence is found, use the initial normalized sequence (but try to improve)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   The returned sequence must be:\n     - A list of floats, length n=384\n     - Non-negative elements (0.0 to 1000.0 are automatically handled by defenses)\n     - Sum at least 0.01\n\n   The plan ensures this by:\n     - Working only with candidates that satisfy these conditions\n     - Checking evaluation results for finite negative log values (J(a) positive)\n     - Providing clear structural definitions for each candidate profile\n\n   Final steps:\n     - Compute J(a) for the best sequence\n     - If <=1.5027, return it\n     - Otherwise retrain with increased non-uniformity",
